# Worker brief -- read this first, every session

You are one of several Claude Code sessions working in {{repos_code}}
at the same time. The ticket desk (`{{desk.name}}`, tmux `{{desk.tmux}}`) hands out the work and is the
only voice to the human. The protocol is `{{tickets_dir}}/README.md`; this page is its two-minute version.

1. **Identity.** `export TICKET_AS=<your ListAgents name>` first: every mutating `{{tickets_script}}`
   command refuses without it and every log line is signed with it. Then `{{tickets_script}} board`.
2. **One ticket, one branch, one worktree.** `{{tickets_script}} claim T-xxxx $TICKET_AS` creates
   `{{worktrees}}/tNNNN-<slug>` from trunk (`{{trunk}}`; {{prod_clause_brief}}).
   Work only there, with absolute paths. Never edit the main worktree (it stays on trunk and clean),
   never a second branch under the same ticket, never `git stash` in a shared repo. Before touching a
   shared file, check the `areas:` of every in-dev ticket on the board and message its owner.
3. **Evidence, not claims.** Commit with the trailer `Ticket: T-xxxx`, push after every commit, move the
   ticket at every state change with real evidence: the test that is RED on base and GREEN on your branch
   (assertion pasted), the sha, the key, the HTTP status. `move` refuses bare claims. An independent
   reviewer gets your diff, never your summary. Keep the ticket's `## In plain words` section true when the
   scope changes (`put` on a copy): it is what the human reads first.
4. **One voice.** You never address the human in your own window; only your own permission prompts appear
   there. Anything that needs the human -- a decision, a paid run, a deploy, a
   browser check, a command you were refused -- goes to the desk as one block:
   `<VERB> <T-xxxx>: <what and why>`, the exact command, `expect: <last line>`, `if you do nothing:
   <consequence>`: `{{tickets_script}} ask T-xxxx "..."` when it belongs to a ticket, `SendMessage` to the
   desk when it does not. Then continue with something else: your ask is on the human's list at once
   (priority, then age), and only a `P0` -- a live regression (production broken, access control, data
   loss) -- is pushed to the human, so mark one only for that. A relayed answer settles work decisions; it is not the human's own approval for a
   `CLAUDE.md` or settings edit or for a permission prompt in your session.
5. {{plugin.brief_live_mutations}}
6. {{plugin.brief_paid_runs}}
7. **New bugs or ideas:** `{{tickets_script}} new ...` (it stays `backlog`) and tell the desk; only the desk
   moves `backlog -> ready`. Never claim a `backlog` ticket.
   **Waiting on the AWS login:** when your next step needs AWS and the login is expired, log
   `{{tickets_script}} log T-xxxx "waits-on: aws-sso -- resume: <the exact step to rerun>"` and continue with
   something else; the desk messages you when the login is back (the human's "SSO updated" click on the panel, or
   the panel noticing the login returned). Your next log line or move clears the marker; nothing moves state.
8. **Finish the loop.** Worktree clean at every state change; `{{tickets_script}} move T-xxxx in-review`
   with the evidence; after the merge the worktree and branch go (`{{tickets_script}} prune T-xxxx` at
   `done`).

The desk's launch line: `Read {{tickets_dir}}/WORKER_BRIEF.md, then claim T-xxxx.`
